1
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:22,200
Traditionally, dot products are

2
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,720
something that's introduced really early

3
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:25,480
on in a linear algebra course, typically

4
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:27,240
right at the start. So, it might seem

5
00:00:27,240 --> 00:00:28,760
strange that I've pushed them back this

6
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:30,160
far in the series.

7
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:31,840
I did this because there's a standard

8
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:33,160
way to introduce the topic which

9
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:34,640
requires nothing more than a basic

10
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:36,680
understanding of vectors, but a fuller

11
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:38,120
understanding of the role that dot

12
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:40,120
products play in math can only really be

13
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:41,640
found under the light of linear

14
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:43,360
transformations.

15
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:44,880
Before that, though, let me just briefly

16
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:46,640
cover the standard way that dot products

17
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:48,520
are introduced, which I'm assuming is at

18
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,160
least partially review for a number of

19
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:51,240
viewers.

20
00:00:51,240 --> 00:00:53,520
Numerically, if you have two vectors of

21
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:55,960
the same dimension, two lists of numbers

22
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:58,120
with the same lengths, taking their dot

23
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:00,480
product means pairing up all of the

24
00:01:00,480 --> 00:01:02,840
coordinates, multiplying those pairs

25
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:06,680
together, and adding the result.

26
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:10,440
So, the vector 1 2 dotted with 3 4 would

27
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:14,520
be 1 * 3 + 2 * 4.

28
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:18,680
The vector 6 2 8 3 dotted with 1 8 5 3

29
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:24,840
would be 6 * 1 + 2 * 8 + 8 * 5 + 3 * 3.

30
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:27,160
Luckily, this computation has a really

31
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:29,480
nice geometric interpretation. To think

32
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:30,920
about the dot product between two

33
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:34,600
vectors V and W, imagine projecting W

34
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:36,480
onto the line that passes through the

35
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:38,640
origin and the tip of V.

36
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:39,960
Multiplying the length of this

37
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,520
projection by the length of V, you have

38
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:46,440
the dot product V.W.

39
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:48,080
Except when this projection of W is

40
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,720
pointing in the opposite direction from

41
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:51,560
V, that dot product will actually be

42
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:53,960
negative.

43
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,200
So, when two vectors are generally

44
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:56,760
pointing in the same direction, their

45
00:01:56,760 --> 00:01:59,520
dot product is positive.

46
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:01,240
When they're perpendicular, meaning the

47
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:03,240
projection of one onto the other is the

48
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:06,000
zero vector, their dot product is zero.

49
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:07,360
And if they point in generally the

50
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:09,160
opposite direction, their dot product is

51
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:11,679
negative.

52
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:13,800
Now, this interpretation is weirdly

53
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:15,760
asymmetric. It treats the two vectors

54
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:17,440
very differently. So, when I first

55
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:19,320
learned this, I was surprised that order

56
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:21,320
doesn't matter.

57
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:23,959
You could instead project V onto W,

58
00:02:23,959 --> 00:02:25,720
multiply the length of the projected V

59
00:02:25,720 --> 00:02:27,720
by the length of W, and get the same

60
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:30,280
result.

61
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:31,840
I mean, doesn't that feel like a really

62
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:34,880
different process?

63
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:37,040
Here's the intuition for why order

64
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:38,480
doesn't matter.

65
00:02:38,480 --> 00:02:40,440
If V and W happen to have the same

66
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:43,000
length, we could leverage some symmetry.

67
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:45,320
Since projecting W onto V, then

68
00:02:45,320 --> 00:02:46,440
multiplying the length of that

69
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:48,680
projection by the length of V, is a

70
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:51,080
complete mirror image of projecting V

71
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:53,360
onto W, then multiplying the length of

72
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:57,160
that projection by the length of W.

73
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:59,640
Now, if you scale one of them, say V, by

74
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:01,560
some constant like two, so that they

75
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:03,920
don't have equal length, the symmetry is

76
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:04,959
broken.

77
00:03:04,959 --> 00:03:06,560
But let's think through how to interpret

78
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:08,480
the dot product between this new vector,

79
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,920
two times V, and W.

80
00:03:10,920 --> 00:03:12,640
If you think of W is getting projected

81
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:16,400
onto V, then the dot product 2V dot W

82
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:19,080
will be exactly twice the dot product V

83
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:20,360
dot W.

84
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:23,200
This is because when you scale V by two,

85
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,680
it doesn't change the length of the

86
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:27,040
projection of W, but it doubles the

87
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:28,519
length of the vector that you're

88
00:03:28,519 --> 00:03:30,360
projecting onto.

89
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:31,720
But on the other hand, let's say you

90
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:33,519
were thinking about V getting projected

91
00:03:33,519 --> 00:03:34,920
onto W.

92
00:03:34,920 --> 00:03:36,440
Well, in that case, the length of the

93
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:38,280
projection is the thing that gets scaled

94
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:40,320
when we multiply V by two, but the

95
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:41,280
length of the vector that you're

96
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:43,720
projecting onto stays constant. So, the

97
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:45,840
overall effect is still to just double

98
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,640
the dot product. So, even though

99
00:03:47,640 --> 00:03:49,600
symmetry is broken in this case, the

100
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:51,440
effect that this scaling has on the

101
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:53,720
value of the dot product is the same

102
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:56,760
under both interpretations.

103
00:03:56,760 --> 00:03:58,520
There's also one other big question that

104
00:03:58,520 --> 00:03:59,880
confused me when I first learned this

105
00:03:59,880 --> 00:04:02,400
stuff. Why on Earth does this numerical

106
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:04,320
process of matching coordinates,

107
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:06,040
multiplying pairs, and adding them

108
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:08,200
together have anything to do with

109
00:04:08,200 --> 00:04:10,840
projection?

110
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:13,120
Well, to give a satisfactory answer, and

111
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:14,480
also to do full justice to the

112
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:16,680
significance of the dot product, we need

113
00:04:16,680 --> 00:04:18,200
to unearth something a little bit deeper

114
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:20,160
going on here, which often goes by the

115
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:21,959
name duality.

116
00:04:21,959 --> 00:04:23,680
But before getting into that, I need to

117
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:25,280
spend some time talking about linear

118
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:27,680
transformations from multiple dimensions

119
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:29,360
to one dimension, which is just the

120
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:32,000
number line.

121
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:34,160
These are functions that take in a 2D

122
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:36,280
vector and spit out some number. But

123
00:04:36,280 --> 00:04:38,080
linear transformations are of course

124
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:39,360
much more restricted than your

125
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:41,320
run-of-the-mill function with a 2D input

126
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:44,040
and a 1D output. As with transformations

127
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:45,560
in higher dimensions, like the ones I

128
00:04:45,560 --> 00:04:47,440
talked about in chapter three, there are

129
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:49,000
some formal properties that make these

130
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:50,680
functions linear. But I'm going to

131
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:52,560
purposely ignore those here so as to not

132
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:54,560
distract from our end goal, and instead

133
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:56,480
focus on a certain visual property

134
00:04:56,480 --> 00:04:57,760
that's equivalent to all the formal

135
00:04:57,760 --> 00:04:59,000
stuff.

136
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,040
If you take a line of evenly spaced dots

137
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:04,240
and apply a transformation,

138
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:06,640
a linear transformation will keep those

139
00:05:06,640 --> 00:05:09,120
dots evenly spaced once they land in the

140
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:12,160
output space, which is the number line.

141
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:14,000
Otherwise, if there's some line of dots

142
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:15,720
that gets unevenly spaced, then your

143
00:05:15,720 --> 00:05:19,160
transformation is not linear.

144
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:21,280
As with the cases we've seen before, one

145
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:23,160
of these linear transformations is

146
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:25,080
completely determined by where it takes

147
00:05:25,080 --> 00:05:27,800
i-hat and j-hat. But this time, each one

148
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:29,680
of those basis vectors just lands on a

149
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:31,720
number. So, when we record where they

150
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:34,480
land as the columns of a matrix, each of

151
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:38,280
those columns just has a single number.

152
00:05:38,280 --> 00:05:41,680
This is a 1 by 2 matrix.

153
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:43,160
Let's walk through an example of what it

154
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:44,280
means to apply one of these

155
00:05:44,280 --> 00:05:46,560
transformations to a vector. Let's say

156
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:48,080
you have a linear transformation that

157
00:05:48,080 --> 00:05:52,400
takes I hat to 1 and J hat to -2.

158
00:05:52,400 --> 00:05:53,640
To follow where a vector with

159
00:05:53,640 --> 00:05:57,040
coordinates, say 4 3, ends up, think of

160
00:05:57,040 --> 00:06:00,200
breaking up this vector as 4 * I hat + 3

161
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:01,760
* J hat.

162
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:04,040
A consequence of linearity is that after

163
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:06,520
the transformation, the vector will be 4

164
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:10,040
* the place where I hat lands, 1, + 3 *

165
00:06:10,040 --> 00:06:12,840
the place where J hat lands, -2,

166
00:06:12,840 --> 00:06:14,400
which in this case implies that it lands

167
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:17,360
on -2.

168
00:06:18,080 --> 00:06:19,680
When you do this calculation purely

169
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:21,600
numerically, it's matrix-vector

170
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:24,480
multiplication.

171
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,760
Now, this numerical operation of

172
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:30,480
multiplying a 1x2 matrix by a vector

173
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:32,120
feels just like taking the dot product

174
00:06:32,120 --> 00:06:34,640
of two vectors. Doesn't that 1x2 matrix

175
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:36,200
just look like a vector that we tipped

176
00:06:36,200 --> 00:06:37,919
on its side?

177
00:06:37,919 --> 00:06:39,280
In fact, we could say right now that

178
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:41,720
there's a nice association between 1x2

179
00:06:41,720 --> 00:06:44,440
matrices and 2D vectors defined by

180
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:46,360
tilting the numerical representation of

181
00:06:46,360 --> 00:06:48,080
a vector on its side to get the

182
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:50,640
associated matrix, or to tip the matrix

183
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:53,480
back up to get the associated vector.

184
00:06:53,480 --> 00:06:54,840
Since we're just looking at numerical

185
00:06:54,840 --> 00:06:56,720
expressions right now, going back and

186
00:06:56,720 --> 00:06:59,240
forth between vectors and 1x2 matrices

187
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:01,720
might feel like a silly thing to do.

188
00:07:01,720 --> 00:07:03,520
But this suggests something that's truly

189
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:05,800
awesome from the geometric view. There's

190
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:07,720
some kind of connection between linear

191
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:09,400
transformations that take vectors to

192
00:07:09,400 --> 00:07:13,840
numbers and vectors themselves.

193
00:07:15,480 --> 00:07:16,880
Let me show an example that clarifies

194
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:18,680
the significance, and which just so

195
00:07:18,680 --> 00:07:20,400
happens to also answer the dot product

196
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:22,000
puzzle from earlier.

197
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:23,560
Unlearn what you have learned, and

198
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:25,280
imagine that you don't already know that

199
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:29,040
the dot product relates to projection.

200
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:30,760
What I'm going to do here is take a copy

201
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:32,320
of the number line and place it

202
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:34,440
diagonally in space somehow, with the

203
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:36,800
number zero sitting at the origin.

204
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:38,400
Now, think of the two-dimensional unit

205
00:07:38,400 --> 00:07:40,560
vector whose tip sits where the number

206
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:42,720
one on the number line is. I want to

207
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:45,680
give that guy a name, u hat.

208
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:47,200
This little guy plays an important role

209
00:07:47,200 --> 00:07:48,920
in what's about to happen, so just keep

210
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:51,000
him in the back of your mind.

211
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,400
If we project 2D vectors straight onto

212
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:55,680
this diagonal number line, in effect,

213
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:57,440
we've just defined a function that takes

214
00:07:57,440 --> 00:07:59,600
2D vectors to numbers.

215
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:01,280
What's more, this function is actually

216
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:03,800
linear since it passes our visual test

217
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:06,200
that any line of evenly spaced dots

218
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:08,240
remains evenly spaced once it lands on

219
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:10,960
the number line.

220
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:13,480
Just to be clear, even though I've

221
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:15,600
embedded the number line in 2D space

222
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:17,400
like this, the outputs of the function

223
00:08:17,400 --> 00:08:19,880
are numbers, not 2D vectors.

224
00:08:19,880 --> 00:08:20,840
You should think of a function that

225
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:22,760
takes in two coordinates and outputs a

226
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:25,000
single coordinate.

227
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,560
But that vector u hat is a

228
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,240
two-dimensional vector living in the

229
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:30,600
input space. It's just situated in such

230
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:32,320
a way that overlaps with the embedding

231
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:34,599
of the number line.

232
00:08:34,599 --> 00:08:36,640
With this projection, we just defined a

233
00:08:36,640 --> 00:08:39,200
linear transformation from 2D vectors to

234
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:40,960
numbers. So, we're going to be able to

235
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:43,159
find some kind of 1 by 2 matrix that

236
00:08:43,159 --> 00:08:45,440
describes that transformation.

237
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:47,800
To find that 1 by 2 matrix, let's zoom

238
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:49,960
in on this diagonal number line setup

239
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:52,440
and think about where i hat and j hat

240
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:54,600
each land since those landing spots are

241
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:58,480
going to be the columns of the matrix.

242
00:08:58,560 --> 00:09:00,120
This part's super cool. We can reason

243
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:01,800
through it with a really elegant piece

244
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:04,560
of symmetry. Since i hat and u hat are

245
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:07,280
both unit vectors, projecting i hat onto

246
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:09,440
the line passing through u hat looks

247
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:11,960
totally symmetric to projecting u hat

248
00:09:11,960 --> 00:09:13,720
onto the x-axis.

249
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:15,440
So, when we ask what number does i hat

250
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:17,480
land on when it gets projected, the

251
00:09:17,480 --> 00:09:18,839
answer's going to be the same as

252
00:09:18,839 --> 00:09:20,760
whatever u hat lands on when it's

253
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:22,839
projected onto the x-axis.

254
00:09:22,839 --> 00:09:25,720
But projecting u hat onto the x-axis

255
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:28,160
just means taking the x-coordinate of u

256
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:31,000
hat. So, by symmetry, the number where i

257
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:32,960
hat lands when it's projected onto that

258
00:09:32,960 --> 00:09:35,000
diagonal number line is going to be the

259
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:39,200
x-coordinate of u hat. Isn't that cool?

260
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:40,920
The reasoning is almost identical for

261
00:09:40,920 --> 00:09:42,720
the j hat case. Think about it for a

262
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:45,280
moment.

263
00:09:49,320 --> 00:09:51,160
For all the same reasons, the y

264
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:53,200
coordinate of u hat gives us the number

265
00:09:53,200 --> 00:09:55,320
where j hat lands when it's projected

266
00:09:55,320 --> 00:09:57,480
onto the number line copy.

267
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:58,760
Pause and ponder that for a moment. I

268
00:09:58,760 --> 00:10:01,000
just think that's really cool.

269
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,800
So, the entries of the 1 by 2 matrix

270
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:05,040
describing the projection transformation

271
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:06,960
are going to be the coordinates of u

272
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:07,840
hat.

273
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:09,280
And computing this projection

274
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:11,200
transformation for arbitrary vectors in

275
00:10:11,200 --> 00:10:13,280
space, which requires multiplying that

276
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:15,240
matrix by those vectors, is

277
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:17,640
computationally identical to taking a

278
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:20,920
dot product with u hat.

279
00:10:21,760 --> 00:10:23,880
This is why taking the dot product with

280
00:10:23,880 --> 00:10:25,920
a unit vector can be interpreted as

281
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:28,400
projecting a vector onto the span of

282
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:32,560
that unit vector and taking the length.

283
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:36,320
So, what about non-unit vectors? For

284
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:37,840
example, let's say we take that unit

285
00:10:37,840 --> 00:10:39,760
vector u hat, but we scale it up by a

286
00:10:39,760 --> 00:10:41,200
factor of three.

287
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:43,400
Numerically, each of its components gets

288
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:45,520
multiplied by three. So, looking at the

289
00:10:45,520 --> 00:10:47,960
matrix associated with that vector, it

290
00:10:47,960 --> 00:10:50,920
takes i hat and j hat to three times the

291
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:54,440
values where they landed before.

292
00:10:55,480 --> 00:10:57,480
Since this is all linear, it implies

293
00:10:57,480 --> 00:10:59,640
more generally that the new matrix can

294
00:10:59,640 --> 00:11:01,600
be interpreted as projecting any vector

295
00:11:01,600 --> 00:11:03,120
onto the number line copy and

296
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:05,240
multiplying where it lands by three.

297
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:07,000
This is why the dot product with a

298
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,480
non-unit vector can be interpreted as

299
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:11,720
first projecting onto that vector, then

300
00:11:11,720 --> 00:11:13,720
scaling up the length of that projection

301
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:16,960
by the length of the vector.

302
00:11:17,760 --> 00:11:18,880
Take a moment to think about what

303
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:20,560
happened here. We had a linear

304
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:22,320
transformation from 2D space to the

305
00:11:22,320 --> 00:11:24,240
number line, which was not defined in

306
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:26,120
terms of numerical vectors or numerical

307
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:27,880
dot products. It was just defined by

308
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:30,080
projecting space onto a diagonal copy of

309
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:31,520
the number line.

310
00:11:31,520 --> 00:11:32,840
But because the transformation is

311
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:35,560
linear, it was necessarily described by

312
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:37,560
some 1 by 2 matrix. And since

313
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:39,680
multiplying a 1 by 2 matrix by a 2D

314
00:11:39,680 --> 00:11:41,480
vector is the same as turning that

315
00:11:41,480 --> 00:11:43,040
matrix on its side and taking a dot

316
00:11:43,040 --> 00:11:45,080
product, this transformation was

317
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:49,720
inescapably related to some 2D vector.

318
00:11:49,720 --> 00:11:51,680
The lesson here is that anytime you have

319
00:11:51,680 --> 00:11:53,600
one of these linear transformations

320
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:55,720
whose output space is the number line,

321
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:57,440
no matter how it was defined, there's

322
00:11:57,440 --> 00:11:59,400
going to be some unique vector V

323
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:01,480
corresponding to that transformation in

324
00:12:01,480 --> 00:12:02,480
the sense that applying the

325
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:04,280
transformation is the same thing as

326
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:08,360
taking a dot product with that vector.

327
00:12:09,880 --> 00:12:12,760
To me, this is utterly beautiful.

328
00:12:12,760 --> 00:12:14,120
It's an example of something in math

329
00:12:14,120 --> 00:12:16,120
called duality.

330
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:18,320
Duality shows up in many different ways

331
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:20,200
and forms throughout math, and it's

332
00:12:20,200 --> 00:12:22,920
super tricky to actually define. Loosely

333
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:24,840
speaking, it refers to situations where

334
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:26,880
you have a natural but surprising

335
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:29,160
correspondence between two types of

336
00:12:29,160 --> 00:12:31,000
mathematical thing.

337
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,240
For the linear algebra case that you

338
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:34,000
just learned about, you'd say that the

339
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,080
dual of a vector is the linear

340
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:38,640
transformation that it encodes.

341
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:40,520
And the dual of a linear transformation

342
00:12:40,520 --> 00:12:42,760
from some space to one dimension is a

343
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:46,680
certain vector in that space.

344
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:49,120
So, to sum up, on the surface, the dot

345
00:12:49,120 --> 00:12:51,560
product is a very useful geometric tool

346
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:53,400
for understanding projections and for

347
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:55,040
testing whether or not vectors tend to

348
00:12:55,040 --> 00:12:57,120
point in the same direction. And that's

349
00:12:57,120 --> 00:12:58,880
probably the most important thing for

350
00:12:58,880 --> 00:13:01,240
you to remember about the dot product.

351
00:13:01,240 --> 00:13:03,240
But, at a deeper level, dotting two

352
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:05,480
vectors together is a way to translate

353
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:06,880
one of them into the world of

354
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:08,400
transformations.

355
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:10,280
Again, numerically, this might feel like

356
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:12,360
a silly point to emphasize. It's just

357
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:14,000
two computations that happen to look

358
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,040
similar. But, the reason I find this so

359
00:13:16,040 --> 00:13:18,200
important is that throughout math, when

360
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:20,040
you're dealing with a vector, once you

361
00:13:20,040 --> 00:13:22,080
really get to know its personality,

362
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:23,760
sometimes you realize that it's easier

363
00:13:23,760 --> 00:13:25,800
to understand it not as an arrow in

364
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:28,880
space, but as the physical embodiment of

365
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:31,160
a linear transformation. It's as if the

366
00:13:31,160 --> 00:13:32,840
vector is really just a conceptual

367
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:35,400
shorthand for a certain transformation,

368
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,120
since it's easier for us to think about

369
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:39,560
arrows in space rather than moving all

370
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:42,960
of that space to the number line.

371
00:13:42,960 --> 00:13:44,600
In the next video, you'll see another

372
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:46,840
really cool example of this duality in

373
00:13:46,840 --> 00:13:48,640
action as I talk about the cross

374
00:13:48,640 --> 00:13:51,320
product.